<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_i-example" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Examples</title>
  
  <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the total internal energy of <m>1.00 \,L</m> of nitrogen (<m>N_2</m>) gas at <m>298 \,K</m> and <m>2 \,atm</m> 
          if its temperature is increased  by <m>10 \,K</m> at constant pressure. What is the energy required to heat <m>1.00 \,mol</m> of nitrogen gas 
          from <m>0 K</m> to <m>298 K</m> at constant pressure?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
         
        <p>
           To estimate the total internal energy of 1.00 L of <m>N_2</m> gas at <m>298 \,K</m> and <m>2 \,atm</m> , 
           we can use the ideal gas law to find the number of moles of nitrogen gas.   we then calculate the internal energy by
          <md>
            dU = n\frac{5}{2} R(T_2-T_1) 
          </md>
          From Ideal gas equation, we can find the number of moles of nitrogen gas: 
          <md>
            n= \frac{PV}{RT} = \frac{(2 \, \text{atm})(1.00 \, \text{L})}{(0.0821 \, \text{L atm/mol K})(298 \, \text{K})} \approx 0.0816 \, \text{mol} 
          </md>
          or, 
            <md>
            \Delta U = 0.0816 \, \text{mol} \times \frac{5}{2} (8.314 \, \text{J/mol K}) (10.0 \, \text{K}) \approx 17.0 \, \text{J} 
          </md>  
          The energy required to heat 1.00 mol of nitrogen gas from 0 K to 298 K at constant pressure can be calculated using the same formula:
          <md>
            \Delta U = 1.00 \, \text{mol} \times \frac{5}{2} (8.314 \, \text{J/mol K}) (298 \, \text{K} - 0 \, \text{K}) \approx 6200 \, \text{J} 
          </md>  
        </p>
      </solution>
     </example>

  

     <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          At <m>T = 300 \, K</m>, <m>1.00 \, mol </m> of <m>CO_2</m> occupies a volume of <m>1.50 \, L</m>. Calculate 
          the pressures given by the ideal gas equation and the van der Waals equation. 
          Given: <m>a = 3.59 L^2 \,atm\,mol^{-2} </m> and <m>b=0.04 /,L mol^{-1} </m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Amount of gas <m>n = 1.00 \, mol</m>; Temperature <m>T = 300 K</m>, Volume <m>V = 1.50 L</m>, 
          Ideal gas constant <m>R = 0.08206 L\cdot atm \cdot mol^{-1}\cdot K^{-1}</m>, van der Waals constant <m>a = 3.59 L^2\cdot atm\cdot mol^{-2} </m>, and
          van der Waals constant <m>b = 0.0427 L\cdot mol^{-1}</m>. Ideal gas equation:
          <md>
            p_{ideal} = \frac{nRT}{V} = \frac{1\cdot 0.08206 \cdot 300}{1.5} =  16.41 \, atm
          </md>
          van der Waal's equations 
          <md>
            \left(p_{vdw}+\frac{an^2}{V^2}\right) \left(V-nb\right) = nRT
          </md>
          <md>
            or, \, p_{vdw}    =  \left(\frac{nRT}{V-nb}\right) -\left( \frac{an^2}{V^2}\right) \\
             =  \left(\frac{1\cdot 0.08206 \cdot 300}{1.5-1\cdot 0.0427}\right)-\left(\frac{3.59 \cdot 1^2}{1.5^2}\right) =15.3 atm.
          </md>
        
        </p>
      </solution>
     </example>
      

     <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The density of dry air at <m>p = 1.0 \, bar</m> and <m>T = 300 \, K</m> is <m>1.161 \,kg m^{-3}</m>. Assuming 
          that it consists entirely of <m>N_2</m> and <m>O_2</m> and using the ideal gas law, determine 
          the amount of each gas in moles in a volume of <m>1 \, m^3</m> and their mole fractions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Pressure <m>p = 1.0 \, bar  = 1.0 \times 10^5 \, Pa  </m>, Temperature  <m>T = 300 \, K</m>, 
          Density <m>\rho = 1.161 \,kg m^{-3}</m>, Total Volume <m>V= 1 \, m^3</m>, Molar Mass of <m>N_2</m> (<m>M_{N_2}) = 0.028013 \,kg \cdot\, mol^{-1} </m>
          Molar Mass of <m>O_2 \, (M_{O_2}) = 0.031999 \, kg \cdot\, mol^{-1} </m> Gas Constant <m>R = 8.314  J\cdot mol^{-1}\cdot K^{-1}</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          From ideal gas equation, <md>
            n_{\text{total }} =\frac{pV}{RT} =  \frac{1.0 \times 10^5 \times 1}{8.314 \cdot 300} =40.091 \,mol.
          </md>
           Total mass of the sample is simply: 
           <md>
            m_{\text{total }} = \rho\times V = 1.161 \times 1 = 1.161\, kg.
           </md>
           The mole fraction is the ratio of the number of moles of a specific component to the total number of moles:
           <md number="yes">
            <mrow xml:id="molefr_eq1"> 
            x_a = \frac{n_a}{n_a+n_b} 
            </mrow>
           </md>
          
           <md>
            m_{\text{total }} = M_{N_2}\cdot n_{N_2} + M_{O_2}\cdot n_{O_2} \\ 
            [\because n=\frac{\text{ mass of substance} }{\text{ Molar Mass}}]
           </md>
           <md number="yes">
            <mrow xml:id="molefr_eq2">
            1.161  = 0.028013 \times n_{N_2} + 0.031999\times n_{O_2} 
            </mrow>
           </md>
            <md number="yes">
              <mrow xml:id="molefr_eq3">
             n_{\text{total }} = 40.091 = n_{N_2} +  n_{O_2} 
             </mrow>
           </md>
           on solving <xref ref="molefr_eq2"/> and <xref ref="molefr_eq3"/>, we get 
           <m>n_{N_2} = 30.583\, mol </m> and <m> n_{O_2} = 9.508 \, mol</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            \therefore \, x_{N_2}= \frac{n_{N_2}}{n_{total}} =  \frac{30.583}{40.091} = 0.763
          </md>
          and 
            <md>
            \therefore \, x_{O_2}= \frac{n_{O_2}}{n_{total}} =  \frac{9.508}{40.091} = 0.237
          </md>
        </p>
       
      </solution>
     </example>


     <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The molecule density of interstellar gas clouds is about <m>10^4</m> molecules/mL. 
          The temperature is approximately <m>10 K</m>. Calculate the pressure. 
          Given: <m>k = 1.3806 \times 10^{-23} J \cdot K^{-1} </m> (or <m>Pa\cdot m^3 \cdot K^{-1}</m>)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The number density of cloud is 
          <md>
            \frac{N}{V} = \frac{10^4 \, molecules}{1\, ml}  \frac{10^4 \, molecules}{10^{-6}\, m^3} = 10^{10} \, molecule/m^3
          </md>
          From ideal gas equation: 
          <md>
            pV = nRT \, \Rightarrow \, pV = NkT \qquad [\because nR=Nk]
          </md>
           <md>
            or, \, p = \frac{N}{V} kT = 10^{10}\times 1.3806 \times 10^{-23} \times 10 = 1.386\times 10^{-12} \, Pa.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
     </example>

     <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p>
                Calculate the amount of gas in moles per cubic meter of atmosphere at <m>p = 1 \, atm</m> 
                and <m>T = 298 \,K</m> using the ideal gas equation.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The atmospheric  content of <m>CO_2</m> is about <m>360 \, ppmv</m> (parts per million by volume). 
          Assuming a pressure of <m>1.00 \, atm</m>, estimate the amount of <m>CO_2</m> in a <m>10.0 \, km</m> 
          layer of the atmosphere at the surface of the Earth. The radius of the Earth is <m>6370 \,km</m>. 
          (The actual amount of <m>CO_2</m> in the atmosphere is about <m>6.0 \times 10^{16} mol</m>.) 
              </p>
            </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
           Given: <m>R =8.314 Pa·m^3/(mol·K) </m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p>
               From ideal gas euqtion: 
                <md>
                  \frac{n}{V} =\frac{p}{RT} = \frac{1.013\times 10^{5}}{8.314 \times 298} = 40.90 mol/m^3.
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Since volume fraction equals mole fraction for ideal gases, 
                mole fraction of <m>CO_2</m>, <m>x_{CO_2} = 360 \, ppm \, \text{ (by volume)} = 360 mol/(10^{-6} m^3) = 3.6\times 10^{-4}</m>
                Now, the volume of atmosphere, 
                <md>
                  V=4\pi \times r^2 \times h =  4\pi \times (6.37\times 10^6)^2 \times 10\times 10^3 = 5.10\times 10^{18}
                </md>
                 </p>
                 <p>
                  Also, total moles of the air in this layer, 
                  <md>
                    n_{\text{total }} =  \frac{n}{V}\times V = 40.90\times 5.10\times 10^{18} = 2.086 \times 10^{20} \, \text{ moles of air}.
                  </md>
                 </p>
                 <p>
                  Moles of <m>n_{CO_2} = x_{CO_2} \times  n_{\text{total }} = 3.6\times 10^{-4}\times 2.086 \times 10^{20} = 7.21\times 10^{16}</m>. 
                 </p>
            </li>
              </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
     </example>

</section>

